# URL Shortener API

A REST API that shortens URLs and tracks click analytics, built with FastAPI and SQLAlchemy.

   Live demo: https://url-shortener-f19f.onrender.com/docs

## Features

- Shorten any http/https URL, with random 7-character codes or custom aliases
- Click analytics: total clicks, clicks per day, top referrers
- Optional link expiration (returns `410 Gone` after expiry)
- Delete links (and their click history)
- Input validation: rejects invalid URLs, `javascript:` links, reserved or duplicate aliases
- 17 automated tests with pytest

## Tech stack

FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, SQLite, Pydantic, pytest, Docker

## API overview

| Method | Endpoint        | Description                                          |
| ------ | --------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| POST   | `/shorten`      | Create a short link (optional `alias`, `expires_at`) |
| GET    | `/{code}`       | Redirect to the original URL (logs a click)          |
| GET    | `/stats/{code}` | Click analytics for a link                           |
| DELETE | `/links/{code}` | Delete a link                                        |

Interactive docs are available at `/docs`.

## Run locally

```bash
git clone https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/url-shortener.git
cd url-shortener
python -m venv venv
venv\Scripts\activate      # macOS/Linux: source venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
uvicorn app.main:app --reload
```

## Run with Docker

```bash
docker build -t url-shortener .
docker run -p 8000:8000 url-shortener
```

## Run the tests

```bash
pytest -v
```

## Design decisions

- **`secrets` instead of `random`** for codes, so they can't be predicted.
- **307 redirects** preserve the HTTP method; codes are indexed for fast lookups.
- **`410 Gone` for expired links**, more precise than 404; **`409 Conflict`** for alias clashes.
- **Dependency injection** (`Depends(get_db)`) lets tests swap in an in-memory database.
- **Timezone handling:** SQLite drops timezone info, so datetimes are normalized to UTC before comparison.

## Limitations and future improvements

- Alembic migrations (currently tables are created with `create_all`)
- PostgreSQL for production, since SQLite on free hosting resets on redeploy
- Rate limiting, user accounts, and Redis caching for hot links
